= Deployment prerequisites =

This repository is organized as MediaWiki source. Deployment must be '''non-destructive by default''' because V2 canon and the V1 provenance archive are intentionally separate.

== Runtime authority ==

Before deployment, reconcile the target installation against:

* [[Special:NamespaceInfo]]
* [[Special:Version]]
* <code>archive-v1/siteinfo.json</code>
* <code>v1-fidelity-audit.json</code>
* [[BITwiki:MediaWiki substrate]]
* [[BITwiki:Programmable knowledge substrate]]

Filesystem folders are source-control projections. They do not create MediaWiki namespaces by existing in Git.

== Required / assumed capabilities ==

The captured BITwiki runtime currently provides the capabilities V2 actively depends on:

* MediaWiki page/revision and template/transclusion system
* Semantic MediaWiki for Properties, Concepts and <code>#ask</code>
* Scribunto for <code>Module:</code> Lua and <code>#invoke</code>
* Cargo for the structured [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]] operational queue
* ParserFunctions
* CategoryTree where <code>&lt;categorytree&gt;</code> is used

Other installed capabilities such as Network, faceted search, PubMed parsing and NCBI taxonomy lookup are available but are not deployment requirements for every page merely because the extensions exist.

Native structured SMW access from Lua through <code>mw.smw</code> is '''not''' assumed merely because Semantic MediaWiki and Scribunto are installed. That interface requires a compatible SemanticScribunto deployment and must be verified before modules depend on it.

== Namespace/source mapping ==

The deployable source-control mappings are recorded in <code>manifest.json</code>.

Important configured extension namespaces include:

* <code>Property:</code> → <code>Property/</code>
* <code>Concept:</code> → <code>Concept/</code>
* <code>Module:</code> → <code>Module/</code>
* <code>smw/schema:</code> → <code>SMWSchema/</code>
* <code>MediaWiki:</code> → <code>MediaWiki/</code>

<code>SMWSchema/</code> uses URL-encoded namespace-local colons in filenames so the repository remains cross-platform.

Nested paths under <code>Module/</code> represent Module subpages. For example, <code>Module/BITwiki/Compiler.lua</code> deploys as <code>Module:BITwiki/Compiler</code>.

== Portal titles are not a confirmed namespace ==

The captured namespace configuration does '''not''' contain a dedicated <code>Portal:</code> namespace.

<code>Portal/</code> is therefore a source-control/title projection for pages whose public titles are of the form <code>Portal:Physics</code>, <code>Portal:Computer science</code>, and so on. Do not infer namespace semantics from the folder.

If a future installation registers a real Portal namespace, that is a deliberate migration/configuration change and must be reflected in [[Special:NamespaceInfo]], the manifest, deployment tooling and navigation tests.

== Do not deploy the archive ==

<code>archive-v1/</code> is repository-only read-only provenance. It contains historical source/revision data and generated reports, not a second live wiki tree.

== Exact V1 preservation boundary ==

The exhaustive archive records 186 public pages / 606 revision bodies, all 9 live templates and callers, all 162 category memberships, all nonnegative namespace inventories and the maintenance reports required for migration validation.

A missing V2 file is therefore '''not''' permission to delete the corresponding live V1 page.

== Template safety ==

The nine live V1 templates are source-controlled directly in <code>Template/</code> for dependency visibility.

V1 <code>Template:Infobox</code> has 80 callers. Do not delete or replace it destructively until those callers have been migrated and verified.

The seven live decorative/header/footer templates still have callers. Their V2 disposition is compatibility/legacy presentation; source control does not make them the new visual standard.

The wanted-template archive contains references to absent implementations. Do not create them automatically during deployment.

== Lua compiler / Module safety ==

The captured live Module namespace was empty. Every V2 module is therefore a new runtime dependency rather than a recovered V1 module.

V2 now treats Lua as the deterministic compiler/type-system/inference-and-projection layer described in [[BITwiki:Lua architecture]]. <code>Module:Structure</code> remains the public validation wrapper used by <code>Template:Knowledge object</code>, but it delegates normalization/type checking into the <code>Module:BITwiki/*</code> family.

Deploy module dependencies before their consumers:

<pre>
Module:BITwiki/Data/Schema
→ Module:BITwiki/Core
→ Module:BITwiki/Compiler
→ Module:Structure
→ Template:Knowledge object
→ knowledge-object consumers
</pre>

<code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> is repository-only authority for compiler-facing controlled vocabularies; MediaWiki cannot execute it directly. <code>Module:BITwiki/Data/Schema</code> is the runtime projection required by Scribunto. Run <code>python scripts/audit_runtime_schema.py</code> before deployment so the JSON authority, Lua projection and repository validator agree.

After import, test valid and deliberately invalid sandbox invocations before broad deployment. Valid V2 knowledge objects should render without validation output; malformed identity should surface an explicit error and maintenance category.

Do not add cross-service credentials, outbound HTTP assumptions, filesystem access, background scheduling, or daemon behavior to Scribunto modules. BIThub/Discourse transport belongs to separately authenticated APIs/plugins/services/agents.

== Semantic MediaWiki safety ==

Deploy Property definitions and required SMW schema/import configuration before pages that assert or query those properties.

After semantic changes, rebuild or refresh Semantic MediaWiki data as required by the installation and verify:

* [[Special:Browse]]
* [[Special:Ask]]
* [[Special:Properties]]
* [[Special:WantedProperties]]
* [[Special:UnusedProperties]]
* [[Special:Concepts]]
* processing / constraint error surfaces

A successful page import with stale semantic data is not a completed semantic deployment.

== Category safety ==

V1 uses 162 category names but had zero authored Category pages at the archived boundary. V2 intentionally creates documented Category pages for selected canonical browse surfaces.

Do not copy all 162 labels into V2 as a flat taxonomy. Preserve the V1 membership archive and apply [[BITwiki:V1 category migration]].

Substantive controlled-Domain categories use [[Template:Knowledge domain category]] to compose stable scope/navigation behavior and derive their knowledge-object table from explicit <code>Domain</code> annotations. Administrative categories remain concise.

== Cargo request workflow ==

Cargo is now used for one bounded repeated-record workflow: [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]]. It does '''not''' replace Semantic MediaWiki as the knowledge graph.

Deployment order matters:

<pre>
Template:Knowledge request
→ create/recreate Cargo table Knowledge_requests
→ BITwiki:Requested knowledge
→ verify Cargo query / drilldown surfaces
</pre>

The <code>#cargo_declare</code> call in [[Template:Knowledge request]] defines the schema and <code>#cargo_store</code> stores each request record. Saving the template alone does '''not''' create or rebuild the database table.

After deploying or changing the template schema, create/recreate <code>Knowledge_requests</code> using either:

* the Cargo '''Create data''' / '''Recreate data''' action from the template page (the <code>?action=recreatedata</code> interface); or
* from the MediaWiki root on modern MediaWiki:

<pre>
php maintenance/run.php Cargo:cargoRecreateData --table Knowledge_requests
</pre>

For a live schema change, prefer Cargo's replacement-table option when continuity of existing queries matters, then switch the replacement in [[Special:CargoTables]]. Depending on server job configuration, the web recreation path may require MediaWiki jobs to run before population completes.

Verify at minimum:

* [[Special:CargoTables]] — <code>Knowledge_requests</code> exists with the declared fields;
* [[Special:Drilldown]] — request lifecycle data is browsable;
* [[BITwiki:Requested knowledge]] — authored tables render and the computed Cargo workflow view returns the same records;
* active requests do not also exist as canonical Main pages.

Repository CI validates the source contract, but it cannot prove that a remote Cargo database table was actually created or populated. That is a deployment verification step.

Do not create additional Cargo tables, subobjects, Network views, or faceted structures solely to increase extension usage. Introduce them only when the data model has a real repeated-record, qualified-fact, or exploration requirement and document migration/dependency consequences first.

== BIThub / Discourse integration boundary ==

The programmable substrate is designed to support BIThub as an interaction plane, but this repository does not by itself deploy the cross-service transport.

Before enabling a BIThub→BITwiki write workflow, verify:

* authentication/authorization identity;
* target MediaWiki API or plugin contract;
* edit actor/process attribution;
* provenance and review behavior;
* failure/retry/idempotency behavior where relevant;
* whether the write targets canonical page content, SMW assertions, Cargo operational state, or another explicit surface.

A BIThub cache, card index, or agent context may project BITwiki state, but it must not silently become a second source of semantic truth.

== CI evidence is not canonical source ==

Validation and audit scripts generate JSON reports during CI. Those files are intentionally untracked and retained as workflow artifacts. The canonical inputs are the reviewed repository source, the generators, and preserved evidence—not a mutable bot-authored report committed after merge.

Neither PR validation nor <code>main</code> validation is authorized to rewrite repository source.

== Safe migration ==

<pre>
1. refresh archive-v1 from the live public wiki
2. review archive-v1/audit.json and v1-fidelity-audit.json
3. run scripts/validate_v2.py, scripts/audit_runtime_schema.py, scripts/audit_substrate.py, and scripts/audit_workflow.py
4. deploy runtime/schema/property/module/template dependencies first
5. create/recreate required Cargo tables before importing their query consumers
6. import/create reviewed V2 pages additively
7. verify rendering, Lua diagnostics, transclusions, categories, SMW data, and Cargo data
8. migrate each V1 source family / callers deliberately
9. move/merge/redirect only after source comparison
10. preserve revision history where technically possible
11. rerun maintenance reports and compare against the pre-migration snapshot
</pre>

== Required post-import checks ==

Review at minimum:

* [[Special:AllPages]]
* [[Special:UncategorizedPages]]
* [[Special:UncategorizedTemplates]]
* [[Special:UnusedTemplates]]
* [[Special:WantedTemplates]]
* [[Special:Categories]] / [[Special:WantedCategories]]
* [[Special:BrokenRedirects]] / [[Special:DoubleRedirects]]
* [[Special:Browse]] / [[Special:Ask]]
* [[Special:Properties]] / [[Special:WantedProperties]]
* [[Special:Concepts]]
* [[Special:CargoTables]] / [[Special:Drilldown]]

A clean migration is not one with zero warnings at any cost. It is one where every remaining warning is understood and intentional.

